{"records":[{"id":"5cdnt4t7xz","title":"Targeting mutant NPM1-expressing AML with T-cell receptor-like antibodies","description":"Abstract\nMutant NPM1 protein accumulation is a hallmark of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Although intracellular proteins are generally inaccessible to antibody-based therapies, proteasomal degradation of mutant NPM1 results in the generation of peptides on the tumor cell surface via major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I molecules. Enhanced presentation of NPM1 mutant A-derived peptides (NPM1mutA283-291-CLAVEEVSL) on HLA-A*02:01-positive tumor cells distinguishes malignant cells from healthy cells. Here, we report the development of an antibody, 2E2, that specifically targets the NPM1 mutant A peptide bound to HLA-A*02:01. We modified 2E2, including chimeric antibodies and bispecific antibodies. Modified antibodies selectively recognize mutant NPM1-expressing cells and mediate antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) in vitro. Moreover, bispecific antibodies derived from 2E2 redirect CD3+ T cells and NK cells to mutant NPM1-expressing cells, suppressing tumor growth both in vitro and in vivo. These findings underscore the potential of NPM1 peptide‒MHC complexes as novel immunotherapeutic targets in AML.\n\nKeywords: TCR-like antibody, Acute myeloid leukemia, Tumor immunotherapy, NPM1, HLA","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-27T15:21:17.055Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5cdnt4t7xz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5cdnt4t7xz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jianda","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Joint Funds for the Innovation of Science and Technology, Fujian Province","grant_id":"2021Y9086, 2022Y4013, 2023Y9153"},{"id":null,"name":"Special Fund from Fujian Provincial Department of Finance","grant_id":"CZBZ202301"},{"id":null,"name":"Natural Science Foundation of Fujian Province","grant_id":"2024J01583, 2022J01240"},{"id":null,"name":"the Talent Scientific Research Project of the First Affiliated Hospital of Fujian Medical University","grant_id":"YJRC4415"},{"id":null,"name":"Scientific Research Foundation of State Key Laboratory of Vaccines for Infectious Diseases, Xiang An Biomedicine Laboratory","grant_id":"2023XAKJ0102057"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82470173, U23A20419, U2005204"},{"id":null,"name":"the Project of Fujian Province Department of Science & Technology","grant_id":"2023Y9079"}],"categories":["Immunotherapy of Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5tj4kymggt","title":"Residual Complex I activity and amphidirectional Complex II operation support glutamate catabolism through mtSLP in anoxia","description":"Anoxia halts oxidative phosphorylation (OXPHOS) causing an accumulation of reduced compounds in the mitochondrial matrix which impedes dehydrogenases. By simultaneously measuring oxygen concentration, NADH autofluorescence, mitochondrial membrane potential and ubiquinone reduction extent in isolated mitochondria in real-time, we demonstrate that Complex I utilized endogenous quinones to oxidize NADH under acute anoxia. 13C metabolic tracing or untargeted analysis of metabolites extracted during anoxia in the presence or absence of site-specific inhibitors of the electron transfer system showed that NAD+ regenerated by Complex I is reduced by the 2-oxoglutarate dehydrogenase Complex yielding succinyl-CoA supporting mitochondrial substrate-level phosphorylation (mtSLP), releasing succinate. Complex II operated amphidirectionally during the anoxic event, providing quinones to Complex I and reducing fumarate to succinate. Our results highlight the importance of quinone provision to Complex I oxidizing NADH maintaining glutamate catabolism and mtSLP in the absence of OXPHOS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-22T08:37:28.545Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5tj4kymggt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5tj4kymggt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dora","last_name":"Ravasz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Bui","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sara","last_name":"Nazarian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gergely","last_name":"Pallag","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Noemi","last_name":"Karnok","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jennie","last_name":"Roberts","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bryan","last_name":"Marzullo","orcid":"0000-0002-1874-6906"},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Tennant","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bennett","last_name":"Greenwood","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alex","last_name":"Kitayev","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Collin","last_name":"Hill","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Timea","last_name":"Komlodi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carolina","last_name":"Doerrier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kristyna","last_name":"Cunatova","orcid":"0000-0001-9993-2040"},{"first_name":"Erika","last_name":"Fernandez-Vizarra","orcid":"0000-0002-2469-142X"},{"first_name":"Erich","last_name":"Gnaiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Kiebish","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexandra","last_name":"Raska","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Krasimir","last_name":"Kolev","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bence","last_name":"Czumbel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Niven","last_name":"Narain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Seyfried","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christos","last_name":"Chinopoulos","orcid":"0000-0003-0183-4149"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hx4nwr8yyd","title":"Data for: Therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia developing 14 years after allogeneic stem cell transplantation, from a persistent R882H-DNMT3A mutated clone of patient origin.","description":"Supplementary (DOCX  2.34MB): \nMaterials and methods. \nFig. S1. TP53 mutation found in the lung carcinoma cells taken from the patient in 2012. \nFig. S2. Karyotype illustrating the metaphases of the 2015 therapy-related myelodysplastic syndrome cells. \nFig. S3. Paint of chromosome 15 (green) by fluorescent in situ hybridization. \nFig. S4. Analysis of the chimerism (Mentype® Chimera® software, Biotype) at 2015 therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis. \nFig. S5. IDH1, DNMT3A and NPM1 mutations found at the time of the acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2001. \nFig. S6. DNMT3A and TP53 mutations found at the time of the therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis in 2015. \nFig. S7. Standard curve for the ASO-qPCR. \nFig. S8. Sensitivity assay for quantitative ASO-qPCR. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-18T12:28:12.179Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hx4nwr8yyd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hx4nwr8yyd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Iván","last_name":"Martín","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Felipe J","last_name":"Chaves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosario","last_name":"Abellán","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francisca","last_name":"García","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mar","last_name":"Tormo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlos","last_name":"Solano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan-Carlos","last_name":"Hernandez-Boluda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María Dolores","last_name":"Olivares","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eva","last_name":"Villamón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fernando","last_name":"Domingo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Blanca","last_name":"Navarro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marisa","last_name":"Calabuig","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paula","last_name":"Amat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Serrano","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cytogenetics"],"journal_issn":["00144800"]},{"id":"2gpjp3mbs6","title":"SETDB1 suppresses NK cell-mediated immunosurveillance in acute myeloid leukemia with granulo-monocytic differentiation","description":"The data include full immunoblot images for \"SETDB1 suppresses NK cell-mediated immunosurveillance in acute myeloid leukemia with granulo-monocytic differentiation\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-15T07:05:55.464Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2gpjp3mbs6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2gpjp3mbs6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yu-Hsuan","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Hematology","Leukemia","Western Blot","Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rm8kjxkkjv","title":"Original blots","description":"Original blots related to \"SUCLG1 Deficiency-Induced Histone Succinylation Impairs Oncogene Expression in Acute Myeloid Leukemia\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-24T16:04:21.305Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rm8kjxkkjv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rm8kjxkkjv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mengqing","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Acute Myelogenous Leukemia","Histone Modification"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"48s882jvnr","title":"Case report-CARE Checklist","description":"Care checklist for case report-Metachronous Presentation of BCL2-Positive Lung Adenocarcinoma and Acute Myeloid Leukemia: a case presentation and review of the literature","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-28T08:34:58.817Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/48s882jvnr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/48s882jvnr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bo","last_name":"Zheng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fstsb2xfsf","title":"PML-regulated mitochondrial metabolism enhances chemosensitivity in human ovarian cancers","description":"High-grade serous ovarian cancer (HGSOC) remains an unmet medical challenge. Here, we unravel an unanticipated metabolic heterogeneity in HGSOC. By combining proteomic, metabolomic, and bioergenetic analyses, we identify two molecular subgroups, low- and high-OXPHOS. While low-OXPHOS exhibit a glycolytic metabolism, high-OXPHOS HGSOCs rely on oxidative phosphorylation, supported by glutamine and fatty acid oxidation, and show chronic oxidative stress. We identify an important role for the PML-PGC-1α axis in the metabolic features of high-OXPHOS HGSOC. In high-OXPHOS tumors, chronic oxidative stress promotes aggregation of PML-nuclear bodies, resulting in activation of the transcriptional co-activator PGC-1α. Active PGC-1α increases synthesis of electron transport chain complexes, thereby promoting mitochondrial respiration. Importantly, high-OXPHOS HGSOCs exhibit increased response to conventional chemotherapies, in which increased oxidative stress, PML, and potentially ferroptosis play key functions. Collectively, our data establish a stress-mediated PML-PGC-1α-dependent mechanism that promotes OXPHOS metabolism and chemosensitivity in ovarian cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-10-10T13:08:27.013Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fstsb2xfsf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fstsb2xfsf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"fatima","last_name":"mechta-grigoriou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yann","last_name":"Kieffer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Geraldine","last_name":"Gentric","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Cancer"],"journal_issn":["15504131"]},{"id":"6phjx8s467","title":"Data for: Enhanced antitumor effects of CD123/CD33 dual-antibody modified liposome targeting to acute myeloid leukemia cells with reduced the antigen-negative escape","description":"A dual-targeting drug delivery system was  developoed to enhance the therapeutic efficacy of chemotherapeutics and reduce the antigen-negative escape for Acute myeloid leukemia. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-07-18T23:35:51.324Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6phjx8s467","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6phjx8s467"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ShengJun","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Delivery","Pharmaceutics","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":["03785173"]},{"id":"rdkg26mfjw","title":"CD37 a safe Chimeric Antigen Receptor target to treat acute myeloid leukemia","description":"CD37 a safe Chimeric Antigen Receptor target to treat acute myeloid leukemia - Raw data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-19T10:40:12.702Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rdkg26mfjw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rdkg26mfjw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sandy","last_name":"Joaquina","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7tzk9r4mn8","title":"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia_2","description":"This dataset contains the raw FACS data of in vivo experiments without CART or AML used to generate the Figures in the paper titled \"Precision Epitope-engineering in Hematopoietic Stem and Progenitor Cells enables CD123 CAR-T cell immunotherapy for acute myeloid leukemia\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-20T06:12:09.186Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7tzk9r4mn8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7tzk9r4mn8"],"contribut